Z||000003||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||12-18-2000||12:05 AM||||I have no clue if you can use the 82 buick 2.5L.  I think, that in 84 the duke was updated to become the "tech-4" found in Fieros.  Maybe, they did not change the block. <BR>But I have doubts.  In 88 they updated the Fiero motor, and almost none of the internal parts seem to be interchangeable.  As far as I can tell, you cannot put an 84-85 head onto an 88 block.  So, at least, it is possible that the 82 block and 84 head are not compatible.  In your shoes, I would be afraid to try to judge this by eye and intuition.  <BR>How about asking for an 84 motor in the Mall of this Forum.  Then maybe you can get another engine that is fully compatible with the 84 ECM (ignition/fuel injection computer) you have in your car already.<BR>Maybe that helps, maybe not . . .||||reg||1
Z||000004||chrissherman||12-18-2000||12:31 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LuckyTheyWereEverMade:<BR><B>I have no clue if you can use the 82 buick 2.5L.  I think, that in 84 the duke was updated to become the "tech-4" found in Fieros.  Maybe, they did not change the block. <BR>But I have doubts.  In 88 they updated the Fiero motor, and almost none of the internal parts seem to be interchangeable.  As far as I can tell, you cannot put an 84-85 head onto an 88 block.  So, at least, it is possible that the 82 block and 84 head are not compatible.  In your shoes, I would be afraid to try to judge this by eye and intuition.  <BR>How about asking for an 84 motor in the Mall of this Forum.  Then maybe you can get another engine that is fully compatible with the 84 ECM (ignition/fuel injection computer) you have in your car already.<BR>Maybe that helps, maybe not . . .</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>In juneau alaska the shiping is out rages And will take 3 weeks to get hear if not longer<BR><p>[This message has been edited by chrissherman (edited 12-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||12-18-2000||02:18 AM||||Sounds like that 82 is a carb'd motor.  Not sure about anything else...<P>The iron duke was used in literally millions of cars during the 80's, shouldn't be too much trouble finding one, even up in alaska.<P>As for the differences from 84-88.. 84-85 were the same, in 86 they switched to roller lifters.  In 87 they switched to a DIS ignition, along with a completly new TBI unit.  Also went to a serpentine V-belt.  In 88 they added a balancer and the oil filter moved to the oil pan.  <P>SFAIK, the heads are the same and interchangeable for 84-88.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||862M4inCA||12-18-2000||08:09 AM||||Standard, I know from a VERY painful experience that the head for an 87 will NOT work on an 86 without MAJOR changes in components as well as wiring. Had to replace the head on my 86 and pulled one from an 87 Celebrity that looked like it had been VERY recently rebuilt. Brought the thing home, installed everything up until I got to the intake manifold (last step) and found that they had moved the water jacket up about 1/2" between the two years. Was VERY noticable when I put the manifold on, bolted , torqued, and THEN noticed that there was a gap between the bottom of the intake ports and the bottom of the manifold. NOT a pleasant experience. ||||reg||1
